1 nitro nitro  6220 Oct  7 12:49 explore-harness-tcp.go\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 61433 Oct  7 12:46 map-v10.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 61556 Oct  7 12:47 map-v11.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 49618 Oct  7 12:07 map-v3.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 57034 Oct  7 12:15 map-v4.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 58664 Oct  7 12:16 map-v5.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 58671 Oct  7 12:20 map-v6.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 59277 Oct  7 12:28 map-v7.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 59908 Oct  7 12:37 map-v8.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 60981 Oct  7 12:43 map-v9.json\n-rw-rw-r--  1 nitro nitro 10625 Oct  7 12:52 submission.json\ndrwxrwxr-x 20 nitro nitro  4096 Oct  7 12:48 tmp\n=== SUBMISSION ===\n{\n  \"action\": \"review\",\n  \"artifact_id\": \"e6f6940adbc9419f91e49143c34d1a84\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Review of the second scenario of obligation c-adopted-term-never-regresses, artifact e6f6940adbc9419f91e49143c34d1a84 (version 1). It supplies the required checker_correspondence item, an applicability item and a resolution for the inherited input issue 9bb534c3914b40c79ce849c715918da5, which applies to this artifact because the earlier resolution covered the first scenario's lineage only. The reviewed execution is check aeb40d35bddd4b2aabdc0491c3fde500: exit 0, completed, prerequisites matched, and the retained stdout shows the node reporting term 6 for the higher-term request while the term context afterwards was 5, with the injected store's write sequence 0,1,1,5,6, the transport reported as tcp and the listener on 127.0.0.1:42919.\",\n  \"review_items\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"e6f6940adbc9419f91e49143c34d1a84\",\n      \"aspect\": \"checker_correspondence\",\n      \"status\": \"no_issue_found\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"s-raft-process-heartbeat\",\n        \"s-net-fastpath\",\n        \"s-net-handle-conn\",\n        \"s-net-set-heartbeat\",\n        \"s-raft-append-term\",\n        \"s-raft-set-current-term\",\n        \"s-state-term-atomic\",\n        \"s-api-fastpath-contract\"\n      ],\n      \"rationale\": \"The obligation under review says that once a node has adopted and persisted term T, no later establishment of the term context may store a lower value, and that this binds every entry point able to update the context. This artifact is the second scenario of that requirement, not a new proposition: it varies the transport only, asking whether the substitute stream layer was what allowed the interleaving. The fixed inputs are the first scenario's harness with both endpoints replaced by the production NewTCPTransport bound to 127.0.0.1, the same armed InmemStore wrapper holding exactly one CurrentTerm write, the same heartbeat-shaped AppendEntries whose classification and callback dispatch are the transport's own code, and the same higher-term AppendEntries sent through the main loop on a second connection. The plan restates the first scenario's legality derivation unchanged (the registration by NewRaft, the shape test in handleCommand, the direct callback invocation on the connection goroutine, and the stable-store-then-memory publication inside setCurrentTerm) and adds the transport kind and local address to the events so the two executions cannot be confused. The oracle is the same comparison with the same scope limit: the context term read through the public accessor must equal the term the node itself reported adopting for the admitted operation, which within this two-write prefix coincides with 'not lower than the admitted term'. The correlation is complete: the admission event carries the node's own report and precedes the result event in the single runner stream with the same node and op identity. The retained observation is the violation: term_advanced with term 6 and success true, then term_context_observed with context_term 5, store_writes 0,1,1,5,6 and transport tcp, with the test exiting 0. Counterevidence was reviewed with the inputs: the prefix is a latency-free loopback rather than a realistic network; the harness skips when the transport cannot bind, which the local construction environment did, so a skip records an unavailable execution and the substitute-layer scenario remains the primary record; the transport is the only variation, with the store latency injection, caller policy and observation unchanged; and the equality encoding's scope limit is stated as before. What the check does not establish is listed in limitations.\",\n      \"counterevidence\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [\n        \"The transport is the production TCP implementation but the network is a latency-free loopback, so a slower or lossy network is not modelled.\",\n        \"A skipped run would record an unavailable execution rather than a result; the substitute-layer scenario remains the primary record for the requirement.\",\n        \"The equality encoding coincides with 'not lower than the admitted term' only inside this two-write prefix.\",\n        \"The scenario varies the transport only; it does not vary the store latency, the caller policy or the observation path.\",\n        \"The event stream reports the transport kind and local address for attribution but does not retain packet-level evidence.\"\n      ],\n      \"challenged_components\": [],\n      \"report_title\": \"生产 TCP 传输同样复现任期回退\",\n      \"report_answer\": \"在两个绑定 127.0.0.1 的真实 NewTCPTransport 之间，节点对更高任期请求回报已采纳任期 6，而随后生效的任期上下文是 5，注入存储写入序列为 0,1,1,5,6，且事件中标记传输为 tcp。测试以退出码 0 结束，违规体现在被判定的任期比较上；这说明内进程替代层并非该交错发生的原因，前缀仍是无延迟回环，跳过则记为不可用执行。\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"e6f6940adbc9419f91e49143c34d1a84\",\n      \"aspect\": \"applicability\",\n      \"status\": \"no_issue_found\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"s-raft-process-heartbeat\",\n        \"s-net-fastpath\",\n        \"s-net-handle-conn\",\n        \"s-net-set-heartbeat\",\n        \"s-raft-append-term\",\n        \"s-raft-set-current-term\",\n        \"s-state-term-atomic\",\n        \"s-api-fastpath-contract\",\n        \"s-raft-vote-record\",\n        \"s-integ-fastpath\"\n      ],\n      \"rationale\": \"The implementation owes the requirement over the production transport for the same reasons as over the substitute one, and with one fewer excuse: the transport is a caller-supplied layer, but the callback contract, the registration, the shape test and the dispatch on the connection goroutine are the library's own, and the production NetworkTransport is the one the library ships and documents as the fast path. The retained execution shows the same violation with that transport, so the substitute layer is not an explanation for the behaviour and the requirement binds the shipped path. The retained condition records the conflict between the comment in raft.go that the handler must only be called from the main thread and the requirement in api.go that the callback must be safe to call concurrently, which earlier reviews answered as an outstanding-repair boundary; the same answer applies here, because the conflict identifies which component must change and not whether the term context may regress. The condition is therefore retained as an independent scope boundary for this artifact as well.\",\n      \"counterevidence\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [\n        \"The duty is established for the loopback prefix and the shipped transport; other networks and other transports that honour the callback remain outside this answer.\",\n        \"The repair location stays open between serialising the callback and making the handler concurrency-safe, as the earlier review recorded.\",\n        \"No latency, loss or reordering is exercised, so the applicability claim is about the shipped transport's dispatch rather than about a fault model.\"\n      ],\n      \"challenged_components\": [],\n      \"report_title\": \"生产传输路径同样被该义务覆盖\",\n      \"report_answer\": \"回调契约、注册、形状判定与连接协程内的直接派发都属于库自身，而 NetworkTransport 正是库随附的快速路径实现，因此该义务覆盖生产传输；保留的条件只是指出应当修复哪一层，并不否任期上下文不得回退的要求。\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"resolutions\": [\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"9bb534c3914b40c79ce849c715918da5\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"s-raft-process-heartbeat\",\n        \"s-net-fastpath\",\n        \"s-net-handle-conn\",\n        \"s-net-set-heartbeat\",\n        \"s-raft-append-term\",\n        \"s-raft-set-current-term\",\n        \"s-state-term-atomic\",\n        \"s-api-fastpath-contract\",\n        \"s-raft-vote-record\",\n        \"s-integ-fastpath\"\n      ],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\n        \"aeb40d35bddd4b2aabdc0491c3fde500\",\n        \"e6f6940adbc9419f91e49143c34d1a84\",\n        \"78e4fd0d22b74afbbe4de6c6a912156d\"\n      ],\n      \"rationale\": \"The condition records that raft.go's processHeartbeat comment demands main-thread-only invocation while api.go requires the registered callback to be safe to call concurrently. The answer is unchanged in substance and now carries a production-transport witness: the registration, the shape test in handleCommand and the direct callback dispatch are the library's own code (s-api-fastpath-contract, s-net-fastpath, s-net-handle-conn, s-net-set-heartbeat, s-raft-process-heartbeat), and the term publication inside the callback is the ordinary setCurrentTerm path whose store write precedes the in-memory update (s-raft-append-term, s-raft-set-current-term, s-state-term-atomic). The execution shows the node reporting term 6 while the context afterwards is 5 over the shipped TCP transport (check aeb40d35bddd4b2aabdc0491c3fde500 of artifact e6f6940adbc9419f91e49143c34d1a84, alongside the substitute-layer check 78e4fd0d22b74afbbe4de6c6a912156d). The conflict is retained as an independent boundary identifying where a repair may go; it does not discharge the obligation, because no reading of the two statements permits the published term to fall below an adopted one.\",\n      \"residual_issue_ids\": [],\n      \"scope_limitations\": [\n        \"The repair location stays open: serialising the callback, making the handler concurrency-safe, or refusing the off-main dispatch are all consistent with the duty.\",\n        \"The resolution covers the shipped transport and the loopback prefix exercised; other networks and transports remain outside it.\"\n      ],\n      \"condition_dispositions\": [\n        {\n          \"condition_id\": \"c-adopted-term-never-regresses/grounding/condition/1\",\n          \"applies_to\": \"independent_scope\",\n          \"rationale\": \"This condition is a conflict between two in-repo statements about who may invoke the heartbeat callback, not a claim about the term value. It is retained as an independent boundary: whichever statement is treated as the contract, the obligation binds the component that publishes the term context, and the observed regression is against a value the node itself reported adopting, now reproduced over the shipped TCP transport.\",\n          \"source_ids\": [\n            \"s-raft-append-term\",\n            \"s-raft-set-current-term\",\n            \"s-state-term-atomic\",\n            \"s-raft-vote-record\",\n            \"s-api-fastpath-contract\",\n            \"s-integ-fastpath\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
